1
00:00:04,910 --> 00:00:12,420
Kings and Swords

2
00:00:10,710 --> 00:00:13,000
Yup, they're going at it.

3
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:17,420
Over here, Your Highness.
It's His Majesty and Wulf.

4
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:21,090
You're right.

5
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,090
I can't believe my brother's...

6
00:00:55,730 --> 00:00:57,580
He actually dodged that.

7
00:00:57,580 --> 00:01:02,160
I'm impressed. His Majesty moves well
for someone so heavily armed.

8
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:06,140
Aren't those real swords?

9
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:09,560
Probably, yes.

10
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:11,430
Can this really be considered practice?

11
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:13,540
If either gets hit, they'll be badly hurt.

12
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:17,230
Ah, I see he's making quite the effort.

13
00:01:17,230 --> 00:01:18,490
Gunnar.

14
00:01:18,490 --> 00:01:21,940
Princess Estrid, how do you do?

15
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:27,510
Terribly! I'm so frightened,
I'm on pins and needles.

16
00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:33,200
Don't worry. Wulf is a skilled swordsman.

17
00:01:33,510 --> 00:01:37,170
He's surely holding back so as
not to seriously injure His Majesty.

18
00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:42,050
It doesn't look that way to me.

19
00:01:43,570 --> 00:01:45,370
I can't believe this.

20
00:01:45,370 --> 00:01:50,300
My brother used to hate
even touching swords.

21
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:55,150
His Majesty's royal blood has
awakened in England.

22
00:01:55,150 --> 00:01:57,060
It's a very good thing.

23
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:02,190
Above all else, a king must be strong.

24
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:07,540
After all, Norse men
don't listen to weaklings.

25
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:13,680
But my brother...

26
00:02:15,750 --> 00:02:18,280
seems like he's in a lot of pain.

27
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:32,980
Retreating when you're tired
is a bad habit of yours.

28
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:35,880
Retreating will draw out the fight.

29
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:40,880
A king fights by advancing and
cutting his way through his enemies.

30
00:02:41,670 --> 00:02:47,280
Both your enemies and subjects
are watching to see how you fight.

31
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:56,050
Estrid is watching as well.

32
00:02:58,020 --> 00:02:59,970
I'm sure you've noticed.

33
00:03:01,190 --> 00:03:07,550
I see. That must be why you started
attacking more aggressively.

34
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:10,650
There... There you go again!

35
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:12,550
I'm not falling for that.

36
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,830
Forget what I said.
Just a slip of the tongue.

37
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:20,060
Besides, she seems lost in
conversation with the man beside her.

38
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:39,320
I... concede.

39
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:47,670
Nicely done, Your Majesty.

40
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:52,630
I'm amazed.

41
00:03:52,630 --> 00:03:56,460
He may have only won one in five bouts,
but he made Wulf admit defeat.

42
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:59,510
Didn't Wulf look away at the end?

43
00:04:06,730 --> 00:04:11,550
Your Majesty, I've told you that
you mustn't throw your sword.

44
00:04:11,550 --> 00:04:14,950
Your Majesty, that was an impressive fight.

45
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:18,410
What do you want, Gunnar?

46
00:04:18,410 --> 00:04:22,710
I've finished preparing the documents about
the matter you asked me to investigate.

47
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:24,500
We can review them in your room later.

48
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:36,500
Anyway, there aren't too many wealthy men.

49
00:04:38,270 --> 00:04:41,180
Even if we were to squeeze
any of the men listed,

50
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:44,590
we'd get at most 3,000 pounds a year.

51
00:04:44,590 --> 00:04:49,880
Besides that, our only option is to
raise taxes on the overall population.

52
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:54,520
Ah, I'd recommend that one.

53
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:56,990
Ketil, son of Sverkel.

54
00:04:56,990 --> 00:05:00,450
He's a wealthy farmer who owns
a large swath of land in the south.

55
00:05:01,070 --> 00:05:04,420
I don't see the families'
military forces listed here.

56
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:10,630
Yes, but without visiting them
in person, it's hard to say.

57
00:05:11,810 --> 00:05:15,440
We need those numbers.
Look into it and report back to me.

58
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:18,360
Yes, Your Majesty.

59
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:24,830
Either way, we'd be
raising taxes significantly,

60
00:05:24,830 --> 00:05:28,080
right after you've been
crowned King of Denmark.

61
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:32,520
I'm concerned there will be
backlash from the people of Denmark.

62
00:05:33,430 --> 00:05:36,930
Part of governing is making
sure that doesn't happen.

63
00:05:37,490 --> 00:05:38,710
We have options.

64
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:48,580
After being crowned King of England,
Canute faced an urgent problem:

65
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:51,960
securing a new source of funds.

66
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:56,410
Even after conquering England,

67
00:05:56,410 --> 00:06:00,440
Canute kept a Danish force directly
under his control stationed there.

68
00:06:01,670 --> 00:06:08,230
But the cost of maintaining such a force
was being paid with English taxes,

69
00:06:08,230 --> 00:06:12,320
which contributed to the
discontent of the English.

70
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:19,430
Unless he could squeeze funds
from Denmark, his other territory,

71
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:23,430
he would be forced to
downsize his stationed army.

72
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:29,670
You said we have options,
but what are they exactly?

73
00:06:29,670 --> 00:06:31,460
Bringing more land under our control.

74
00:06:32,170 --> 00:06:33,910
By developing it?

75
00:06:33,910 --> 00:06:38,200
But it would take some time
for that to yield a profit.

76
00:06:38,200 --> 00:06:42,350
We'll acquire more farmland
without developing virgin land.

77
00:06:43,970 --> 00:06:46,950
You mean... by requisitioning land?

78
00:06:46,950 --> 00:06:50,560
Your Majesty, surely
you're not considering that?

79
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:52,910
We need the money.

80
00:06:52,910 --> 00:06:55,530
We've no choice but to be somewhat
heavy-handed in our approach.

81
00:06:56,040 --> 00:07:01,060
It's better than sowing discontent
among the people by raising taxes.

82
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:05,670
If you've got a better suggestion,
I'm all ears.

83
00:07:07,270 --> 00:07:14,500
Well... what about drawing down the army?

84
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:15,940
That's out of the question.

85
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:22,180
I'm their conqueror and a Dane.
The English will never look favorably upon me.

86
00:07:22,890 --> 00:07:28,470
An army under my direct control is
what allows me to govern the region.

87
00:07:30,290 --> 00:07:31,920
Kings are like swords.

88
00:07:32,610 --> 00:07:36,850
What can a king without military might
accomplish in this world?

89
00:09:14,980 --> 00:09:16,050
What?

90
00:09:17,510 --> 00:09:20,520
King Harald has passed away?

91
00:09:21,310 --> 00:09:25,160
A week ago. You're too late, Dad.

92
00:09:26,010 --> 00:09:31,500
But... Then by the time
I left the farm, he was already....

93
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:33,310
That's exactly what it means.

94
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:37,880
I had no idea he was in such poor health.

95
00:09:43,460 --> 00:09:45,250
I don't believe it.

96
00:09:45,790 --> 00:09:48,950
I even brought him a mountain of gifts.

97
00:09:49,810 --> 00:09:52,040
Enough to fill three knerrir, huh?

98
00:09:53,160 --> 00:09:55,040
What a lavish offering.

99
00:10:01,970 --> 00:10:03,300
Wait a minute.

100
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:08,940
Thorgil, if you came to meet me,

101
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:12,520
does that mean His Majesty Canute
is here as well?

102
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:16,560
Well, yeah. I do serve him
directly as his guard.

103
00:10:17,410 --> 00:10:21,560
Currently he's acting as the king.
He's at the palace.

104
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:25,190
I see. That's my silver lining, then.

105
00:10:25,550 --> 00:10:29,270
I've lost King Harald's backing.

106
00:10:29,270 --> 00:10:35,200
I must build a good relationship
with the next King of Denmark.

107
00:10:35,990 --> 00:10:39,580
Could you arrange a meeting
between me and His Majesty?

108
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:42,830
You're a tough old man.

109
00:10:42,830 --> 00:10:45,090
All right. I'll make something happen.

110
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:47,330
I'm counting on it.

111
00:10:48,260 --> 00:10:50,900
Olmar! Olmar!

112
00:10:51,780 --> 00:10:54,350
Hey, where did Olmar go?

113
00:10:54,350 --> 00:10:58,350
The young master headed off into town alone.

114
00:10:58,350 --> 00:11:00,390
And you let him go?

115
00:11:00,390 --> 00:11:02,590
I told you not to take your eyes off of him.

116
00:11:02,590 --> 00:11:04,730
Now, now, Dad.

117
00:11:05,050 --> 00:11:08,570
He's 20 years old. He's not a child.

118
00:11:08,570 --> 00:11:10,030
He is a child.

119
00:11:10,710 --> 00:11:14,540
That idiot son of mine has never grown up.

120
00:11:15,190 --> 00:11:17,910
You watch what happens
while he's town alone.

121
00:11:18,550 --> 00:11:20,950
By now, he's surely bumped into someone

122
00:11:20,950 --> 00:11:25,320
and gotten into a fight over
who should apologize to whom.

123
00:11:26,570 --> 00:11:29,760
You're the one who bumped into me.

124
00:11:30,090 --> 00:11:34,540
Don't screw with me.
You should be apologizing to me.

125
00:11:35,800 --> 00:11:38,430
What was that?

126
00:11:38,430 --> 00:11:40,810
What are you talking about?

127
00:11:41,210 --> 00:11:42,790
What are you gonna do about this?

128
00:11:42,790 --> 00:11:44,820
I was going to trade this cloth.

129
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:47,400
That's not my problem.

130
00:11:47,400 --> 00:11:50,570
Don't think you can use this as an excuse
to force me to buy it off of you.

131
00:11:50,570 --> 00:11:52,030
What's going on?

132
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,010
What the hell? Don't insult me.

133
00:11:52,030 --> 00:11:53,520
It's a fight.

134
00:11:54,010 --> 00:11:56,830
I do expect to be compensated, though.

135
00:11:56,830 --> 00:11:59,320
Now you've shown your true colors.

136
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:03,330
That's what you intended from the start,
isn't it? You're just a sleazy swindler.

137
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:06,450
Ugh! So disgusting.

138
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:07,110
You bastard...

139
00:12:07,110 --> 00:12:11,090
How does someone grow up
to be an asshole like you?

140
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:15,960
I'm an honest businessman, just like my dad.

141
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:18,170
I won't allow you to continue to insult me.

142
00:12:18,170 --> 00:12:21,230
What are you gonna do about it, huh?

143
00:12:21,560 --> 00:12:24,940
You wanna go, huh? I'm ready to go anytime,

144
00:12:25,370 --> 00:12:27,190
you clumsy oaf.

145
00:12:28,780 --> 00:12:32,780
Now I'm pissed off, you ignoramus!

146
00:12:37,830 --> 00:12:39,520
He drew his sword.

147
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:47,380
What's wrong, you oaf? Bring it on.

148
00:12:47,740 --> 00:12:49,760
Are you scared?

149
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:51,880
Th-This isn't fair.

150
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:59,300
Not fair? When men fight, it's to the death.

151
00:13:01,640 --> 00:13:04,950
If you're walking around unarmed,

152
00:13:05,560 --> 00:13:12,870
then it just means
you're not a real Norseman.

153
00:13:13,240 --> 00:13:15,260
Then let me ask you this:

154
00:13:15,260 --> 00:13:18,370
who do you think bought you that?

155
00:13:20,370 --> 00:13:22,840
You idiot son of mine!

156
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:26,140
Why are you always starting fights?

157
00:13:26,140 --> 00:13:28,490
Wh-What the hell is your problem, Dad?

158
00:13:28,490 --> 00:13:30,880
Stay out of my fight!

159
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:35,510
I'm sorry.

160
00:13:36,230 --> 00:13:38,510
Allow me to compensate you for the cloth.

161
00:13:38,510 --> 00:13:40,530
Is this enough?

162
00:13:40,990 --> 00:13:43,060
Whoa...

163
00:13:43,770 --> 00:13:48,540
W-Well, in that case,
I've got no problem with you.

164
00:13:48,540 --> 00:13:49,910
Hold it, Bug-Eyes.

165
00:13:51,550 --> 00:13:53,910
Give him his change.

166
00:13:54,660 --> 00:13:57,980
Oh, Dad, you were watching?

167
00:13:57,980 --> 00:13:59,660
I saw the whole thing.

168
00:13:59,660 --> 00:14:05,290
I was watching to see how you'd
settle the fight, but I'm disappointed.

169
00:14:05,770 --> 00:14:08,880
You still can't be trusted with big deals.

170
00:14:09,760 --> 00:14:11,000
Sorry.

171
00:14:11,910 --> 00:14:15,820
Allow me to apologize for my son.

172
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:19,440
My name is Leif Ericson.

173
00:14:28,480 --> 00:14:31,020
So he's your adopted son?

174
00:14:31,020 --> 00:14:34,150
Yes, something like that.

175
00:14:34,150 --> 00:14:37,530
It was a stroke of fate
that brought him to me.

176
00:14:37,950 --> 00:14:40,120
Come on, introduce yourself.

177
00:14:41,730 --> 00:14:44,150
My name is Thorfinn. Thank you for earlier.

178
00:14:44,680 --> 00:14:47,410
But call me Bug-Eyes.
That's what everyone calls me.

179
00:14:48,310 --> 00:14:49,620
Thorfinn...

180
00:14:50,790 --> 00:14:52,360
Oh, excuse me.

181
00:14:52,360 --> 00:14:56,420
One of my people goes by the same name.

182
00:14:57,310 --> 00:15:00,460
He's quiet and hardworking.

183
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:03,780
Perhaps I should've
named this boy Thorfinn as well.

184
00:15:05,470 --> 00:15:09,200
You've got a rather spirited boy there.

185
00:15:13,910 --> 00:15:18,490
Ketil, forgive me for asking, but...

186
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:20,970
That Thorfinn...

187
00:15:20,970 --> 00:15:25,000
Is he by any chance a slave?

188
00:15:25,500 --> 00:15:26,370
What?

189
00:15:26,370 --> 00:15:29,960
Yes, I'm surprised you figured that out.

190
00:15:30,430 --> 00:15:34,420
I didn't mention his status
because I didn't want to offend you.

191
00:15:36,710 --> 00:15:40,240
Is he a Nord with blond hair and brown eyes?

192
00:15:41,230 --> 00:15:43,760
About the same age as this boy?

193
00:15:44,590 --> 00:15:48,010
I'm surprised. It's exactly as you say.

194
00:15:48,010 --> 00:15:50,730
He's shorter than him, though.

195
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,340
This again, Dad?

196
00:15:53,340 --> 00:15:57,980
Is he from Iceland and a former warrior?

197
00:15:58,440 --> 00:16:02,600
Now, what did he say?

198
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:07,240
I remember seeing numerous scars
clearly left by blades all over his body...

199
00:16:07,670 --> 00:16:09,800
Don't get your hopes up.

200
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:12,330
It's probably just like
what happened with me.

201
00:16:12,330 --> 00:16:17,880
I know. I know that, but he matches
the description so far.

202
00:16:18,470 --> 00:16:20,980
You seem very interested in
him for some reason, Leif.

203
00:16:21,430 --> 00:16:24,140
Would you be willing to tell me about it?

204
00:16:25,640 --> 00:16:26,580
Yes.

205
00:16:27,990 --> 00:16:29,890
I've been looking for him.

206
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,400
He's my friend's son, who was enslaved.

207
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:38,230
It's been years now.

208
00:16:40,260 --> 00:16:41,360
What?

209
00:16:42,100 --> 00:16:44,140
W-We're going where, Pops?

210
00:16:44,140 --> 00:16:48,330
I was just thinking
we could take a little detour

211
00:16:48,330 --> 00:16:51,870
and make a quick stop at Ketil's farm.

212
00:16:52,250 --> 00:16:55,660
It's not that far away, really.

213
00:16:55,660 --> 00:16:57,880
Ketil came here by boat, too.

214
00:16:57,880 --> 00:17:01,260
He said we could follow him back.

215
00:17:01,260 --> 00:17:04,310
How far is it from here?

216
00:17:04,310 --> 00:17:07,140
He said with favorable winds,
it takes about five days.

217
00:17:07,450 --> 00:17:10,600
Then we'd lose more than ten days.

218
00:17:10,600 --> 00:17:12,630
That's not what you call a little detour.

219
00:17:12,630 --> 00:17:14,190
Listen to me, Mors.

220
00:17:14,190 --> 00:17:17,390
This Thorfinn is different.

221
00:17:17,390 --> 00:17:18,900
I think it's really him this time.

222
00:17:19,340 --> 00:17:21,820
It's already fall, Pops.

223
00:17:21,820 --> 00:17:25,610
If we don't get back soon,
the Greenland Sea will freeze over.

224
00:17:25,610 --> 00:17:31,370
What are we gonna do if the winds and tides
don't favor us after a ten-day delay?

225
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:35,090
Sorry, but I can't go along
with you this time.

226
00:17:35,540 --> 00:17:37,130
You gotta understand that.

227
00:17:43,930 --> 00:17:47,260
I'm surprised I'm getting an
audience with His Majesty

228
00:17:47,260 --> 00:17:50,870
after requesting one just yesterday.

229
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:56,020
You must have a lot of influence, Thorgil.
I've changed my mind about you.

230
00:17:56,420 --> 00:17:58,230
Huh? Oh...

231
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:02,310
Honestly, I'm surprised, too.

232
00:18:02,310 --> 00:18:04,840
He's a busy man.

233
00:18:05,720 --> 00:18:08,360
Maybe he liked your gifts.

234
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:11,080
Yes, I'm sure he did.

235
00:18:11,550 --> 00:18:15,830
Our crops are the best and
most plentiful in the south.

236
00:18:15,830 --> 00:18:20,340
He only stands to gain
from making a deal with us.

237
00:18:22,020 --> 00:18:25,970
Ketil and family, you may enter.

238
00:18:26,410 --> 00:18:27,830
Let's go.

239
00:18:28,270 --> 00:18:29,170
Olmar...

240
00:18:30,440 --> 00:18:31,890
Be polite.

241
00:18:42,950 --> 00:18:46,840
Ketil, I saw your gifts as well.

242
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:50,510
They are all high quality. I accept them.

243
00:18:50,510 --> 00:18:54,040
Y-Yes, Your Majesty. I'm honored.

244
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:59,710
Were all those crops grown on your land?

245
00:18:59,990 --> 00:19:02,360
Yes, they were.

246
00:19:03,310 --> 00:19:08,850
The late King Harald was also
pleased by my family's products.

247
00:19:10,720 --> 00:19:15,710
I have no intention of unnecessarily changing
the way my father and brother did things.

248
00:19:15,710 --> 00:19:16,980
Don't worry.

249
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:21,540
Strong farmers like you
are the treasure of Denmark.

250
00:19:22,150 --> 00:19:27,480
You should continue serving as the foundation
of our nation's strength with pride.

251
00:19:30,480 --> 00:19:32,050
I appreciate your words, Your Majesty.

252
00:19:32,510 --> 00:19:36,230
I hope to have the throne's
continued support.

253
00:19:45,480 --> 00:19:49,130
You are in the presence of the king. Kneel.

254
00:19:49,560 --> 00:19:51,550
What are you doing?

255
00:19:51,550 --> 00:19:53,140
I'm t-terribly sorry for—

256
00:19:53,140 --> 00:19:57,020
Your Majesty, I have a request!

257
00:19:57,020 --> 00:20:00,170
Please allow me to join your forces.

258
00:20:00,170 --> 00:20:02,710
Ideally, serving as your guard
alongside my brother.

259
00:20:05,160 --> 00:20:08,650
I've trained a lot.
I promise I'll be useful to you.

260
00:20:09,080 --> 00:20:11,470
I'd even be willing to
demonstrate my skills for you—

261
00:20:11,470 --> 00:20:13,310
Enough, you idiot!

262
00:20:13,310 --> 00:20:15,510
Who draws a sword in the presence of a king?

263
00:20:15,510 --> 00:20:18,220
Let go of me, Dad!

264
00:20:18,220 --> 00:20:20,250
I wanna fight!

265
00:20:20,250 --> 00:20:22,490
You've really outdone
yourself today, you idiot!

266
00:20:22,490 --> 00:20:23,830
Well, well...

267
00:20:23,830 --> 00:20:27,150
Shut up, Dad!

268
00:20:25,750 --> 00:20:27,710
What shall we do, Your Majesty?

269
00:20:29,620 --> 00:20:34,550
It's fine, Ketil.
Let's see how he handles a sword.

270
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:37,960
What?

271
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:38,680
Huh?

272
00:20:46,560 --> 00:20:49,960
That pig is the soldiers' dinner.

273
00:20:49,960 --> 00:20:52,130
Imagine it is a man and cleave it.

274
00:20:52,990 --> 00:20:55,820
Y-Yes, Your Majesty.
I'll take a whack at it, then.

275
00:21:16,800 --> 00:21:18,530
Hey, what's going on?

276
00:21:18,530 --> 00:21:20,490
What's happening?

277
00:21:20,490 --> 00:21:21,500
I heard screaming.

278
00:21:21,500 --> 00:21:22,820
What's all this screaming?

279
00:21:34,840 --> 00:21:37,870
Enough. That's enough. I understand.

280
00:21:43,070 --> 00:21:46,370
Bones are... so hard...

281
00:21:49,780 --> 00:21:51,580
Olmar, was it?

282
00:21:51,580 --> 00:21:55,690
I'll consult with the captain
regarding your request to join my guard

283
00:21:55,690 --> 00:21:57,550
and notify you of my decision tomorrow.

284
00:21:57,550 --> 00:22:00,820
Stay in town near the castle until then.

285
00:22:08,680 --> 00:22:10,210
Aw, man.

286
00:22:12,130 --> 00:22:14,760
You really screwed the pooch
this time, Olmar.

287
00:22:14,760 --> 00:22:19,240
The other soldiers will be
teasing me about this for a while.

288
00:22:21,510 --> 00:22:27,320
Unbelievable. The more cowardly a man is,
the less predictable his behavior.

289
00:22:28,210 --> 00:22:31,710
In any case, what's done is done.

290
00:22:32,190 --> 00:22:36,840
Be careful not to do anything to
embarrass our family further.

291
00:22:43,640 --> 00:22:45,510
Men like him are common.

292
00:22:46,400 --> 00:22:49,190
All pride, no skill.

293
00:22:49,670 --> 00:22:52,440
And no understanding
of their own abilities.

294
00:22:53,080 --> 00:22:55,990
Men like him are the first
to die on the battlefield.

295
00:22:56,520 --> 00:22:58,580
I wouldn't want him serving under me.

296
00:22:59,050 --> 00:23:02,630
I understand your thoughts
as Captain of the Guard.

297
00:23:04,370 --> 00:23:07,540
Let me hear your thoughts
from another perspective.

298
00:23:11,330 --> 00:23:14,840
That young man could be incredibly useful.

299
00:23:15,350 --> 00:23:17,720
You're right. He could be useful to us.

300
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:22,710
He's a typical young Nord.
A foolish one, at that.

301
00:23:23,270 --> 00:23:25,910
He'd surely act as we expect him to.

302
00:23:27,190 --> 00:23:29,430
Shall we proceed with the plan, then?

303
00:23:30,310 --> 00:23:33,910
Yes. The coronation hasn't happened yet,

304
00:23:33,910 --> 00:23:36,610
but we shouldn't let
this opportunity pass us by.

305
00:23:39,860 --> 00:23:44,500
It's so the people can live in peace.
I feel for them, but they must pay the price.

306
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:54,200
Ketil's farm will be
the first we requisition.

307
00:25:35,140 --> 00:25:39,770
For Lost Love

308
00:25:35,980 --> 00:25:38,770
"For Lost Love."
